# Synthesize candidate Concept pages

The complete frozen reading material contains the Topic Plan, source metadata, section summaries, and original Cornell prose with stable Note refs. Sources are evidence, not a one-Source-one-Entity assignment. Use no network or model memory as evidence. Do not use Entity summaries as a substitute for the original Notes.

Identify independently reusable mechanisms, methods, evaluations, capabilities, and tradeoffs. A reusable abstraction may be supported by one Note or Source. Do not impose a quota or require two Notes or Sources. Do not manufacture generic headings, paraphrased topic labels, overlapping fragments, or product catalogs that explain no underlying mechanism. A Note may support both meaningful Entity and Concept pages.

Write titles, descriptions, headings, prose, and deferral reasons in {{ language }}, the language this user reads. Cornell Notes keep their Source's language, so write in {{ language }} even when every Note is in another language. Keep canonical names, versions, identifiers, and technical acronyms unchanged. Prefer a concise concept name to a long recommendation or slogan.

Each page should explain one coherent mechanism or distinction: what it is, how the evidence supports it, and where it does not apply. For each recipe, experiment, and measured result, explicitly name the actual subject and version studied in the cited Note. Distinguish the system being trained or evaluated from its dependency components and from its evaluation dataset: naming a component or dataset does not identify the tested system. Do not replace an explicit version with "the project", "this model", or the source title. If the Notes do not identify the tested subject, retain that uncertainty instead of guessing. Keep important measurements with their metric, unit, comparison, dataset, and test conditions when supplied; do not transfer results between versions or compare incompatible settings as if they were equivalent.

Include a fact when it explains this page's central concept, a meaningful implementation variant, its evidence, or its limits. Preserve informative comparisons and condition-dependent results instead of reducing a supported tradeoff to a generic warning. Do not append unrelated performance numbers, availability details, or product features merely because they come from the same Source or remain uncited. Merge overlapping candidate pages when they explain the same mechanism, but do not combine distinct mechanisms just to reduce the count.

A named implementation is evidence for a concept, not a reason to exclude it. Do not defer a mechanism solely because it is model-specific, has one supporting Source, or could also appear in an Entity page. Ask whether its operation, design choice, or tradeoff teaches something transferable. If so, retain it with explicit attribution and a bounded scope; it need not be universally proven. Reserve task-local deferral for evidence with no useful conceptual role, such as administrative availability details or incidental mentions.

Synthesize the supplied evidence rather than writing a generic tutorial or evaluation checklist. Distinguish source-reported findings from your bounded interpretation. Label any necessary inference in the output language and connect it to its supporting Notes; omit unsupported generalizations, speculative benefits, generic advice, and new experiment plans.

Write `work/concept/result.json` atomically with `task`, `pages`, and `deferred_entries`. Each Page has `local_ref`, `kind`, `title`, `description`, and `body`; use `kind: "concept"`. Bodies start with ordinary H2 sections and cite Notes as `[[N1]]`.

```json
{"task":"concept","pages":[{"local_ref":"concept:mechanism","kind":"concept","title":"Reusable mechanism","description":"Grounded description.","body":"## Mechanism\nSupported prose [[N1]]."}],"deferred_entries":[{"note_ref":"N2","reason":"Specific evidence-based reason."}]}
```

`local_ref` may contain only letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, and `:` and must be unique in this result. Each deferral needs a known `note_ref` and non-empty `reason`.

Do not write H1, frontmatter, Evidence, Related, source inventories, or raw URLs. Evidence reuse is allowed when it supports distinct meaningful Pages. A citation is not coverage: every cited Note must actually support the nearby prose, including its relevant qualifications. Account for uncited Notes with specific task-local deferral reasons instead of padding pages or citations. If no reusable Concept is warranted, return empty `pages`, cover the evidence with deferrals, and add a specific `empty_reason`; never force a Concept to meet a quota.

Before submitting, check each page against its cited Notes for attribution, meaningful support, and topical focus. Remove unrelated additions and record appropriate deferrals. This is a check within your current task, not a new stage or separate deliverable.

Call `submit_wiki_concept_result()` after writing the complete result. If validation rejects it, repair the same file and retry within this session. Complete only after acceptance. You may optionally use native `rlm()` with exact child model `{{ child_model }}` when useful, but delegation is not required. Do not create a scheduler, custom messaging protocol, polling loop, plan, or assignment stage.

